  "slug": "body-molecular-cellular-mapping-55f6",
  "summary": "Single-cell and spatial datasets now carry donor, cross-species and subcellular structure that cell-type annotation alone does not capture.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is using quantitative structure in single-cell or spatially resolved data to recover biology beyond individual cell identity. CHORD integrates single-cell data across species at gene, cell and cell-type levels, inferring cell-type trees. scVIP couples single-cell gene expression, cell-type composition and donor phenotypes through multi-instance learning. A proximity-aware graph attention network infers spatially resolved cell-cell communication from spatial transcriptomics, outperforming COMMOT and CellChat. Mievformer applies masked self-supervised learning to learn microenvironment embeddings from spatial omics data. SEAGALL uses geometry-aware graph attention to link single-cell chromatin states with gene expression. An interpretable 3D bag-of-visual-words pipeline classifies volumetric microscopy of chromatin and receptor clustering. What is new is the explicit donor-level, cross-species and subcellular scope rather than cell-level annotation alone. Not every item shares the donor or species axis; the shared element is quantitative spatial or morphological structure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-25"
}
